EXPERIMENT 6
Date: Not specified
Title: To study the MD5 hashing algorithm and understand its data transformation process.
Requirements
• Computer or smartphone with internet access
• Virtual Labs website
• MD5 hashing experiment
Theory
MD5 (Message Digest Algorithm 5) is a cryptographic hash function that converts an input message of arbitrary
length into a fixed-length hash value of 128 bits.
The hash value is represented by 32 hexadecimal characters.
MD5 is used for generating message digests and checking file integrity. However, it is no longer considered
secure for security-sensitive applications because it is vulnerable to collision attacks.
A small change in the input generally produces a significantly different hash value. This property is known as the
avalanche effect.
Procedure
1. Open the Virtual Lab website.
2. Navigate to Computer Science and Engineering → Introduction to Cyber Security Lab → Experiments.
3. Select Data Transformation within MD5 Hashing Algorithm.
4. Read the instructions provided on the experiment page.
5. Enter the required input message in the designated field.
6. Execute the experiment using the provided control.
7. Observe the data transformation steps and the generated MD5 hash value.
8. Change the input message slightly and execute the experiment again.
9. Compare the generated hash values and note the differences.
10. Record the observations and results.
Observation
The MD5 algorithm converts the input message into a fixed-length hash value of 128 bits, represented by 32
hexadecimal characters.
Changing even a single character in the input generally results in a different hash value. This property is known
as the avalanche effect.
Result
The data transformation process of the MD5 hashing algorithm was studied. It was observed that MD5 generated
a fixed-length 128-bit hash digest regardless of the input message length.
